My specific actual use case is to have a Sequencer producing events with near perfect timing-accuracy, while at the same time have those events, except for the one event currently
being scheduled i.e. the event 'under playhead', to be modifiable on the fly (while the Sequencer is actively scheduling them in the `scheduler_` thread). So that's why I'm searching for
 ways to give the `scheduler_` thread lock-free (or as close to lock-free as is safe) priority access to the Sequencer's `events_` container. If it can't be done lock-free, then at least
 I want the on-the-fly updates to never interfere with the performance of the `scheduler_` thread, so I never want the scheduler thread to be waiting on locks held by on-the-fly update
functions, while on the other hand I'm perfectly fine with the on-the-fly update functions having a small performance penalty because they are waiting on locks held by the scheduler
thread or on lock-free thread-safety mechanisms used by the scheduler thread. I want my scheduler thread to be able to do 2 things without any competition from other threads: 1. read the
 next event from `events_` so that it can get the duration and 2. push that event to the output queue `output_`.

● Excellent! Now we can design a proper solution. Your use case is a reader-priority (scheduler-priority) problem with very specific access patterns. This is actually more tractable than
   general concurrent iteration.

  Your Key Constraints (Perfect for Optimization)

  1. Scheduler thread: Sequential access (reads events in order)
  2. Scheduler thread: Only needs to read ONE event at a time
  3. Control threads: Can wait/block - no problem
  4. Protection rule: Event "under playhead" is off-limits to control threads

  Recommended Solution: SeqLock Pattern

  This is exactly what you need. It's used in the Linux kernel for read-mostly data with these properties:

  - Readers (scheduler) NEVER block - completely lock-free
  - Readers are wait-free - bounded time, no retry loops needed if used correctly
  - Writers (control) may block each other - but not readers
  - Readers always get consistent data - even during writes

  How SeqLock Works

  class Sequencer {
  private:
      std::vector<Event> events_;
      std::atomic<size_t> playhead_{0};

      // SeqLock components
      std::atomic<uint64_t> seq_{0};  // Sequence counter
      std::mutex write_mutex_;        // Writers serialize with each other

  public:
      // SCHEDULER THREAD - LOCK-FREE READ
      Event get_current_event() const {
          uint64_t seq1, seq2;
          Event event_copy;

          do {
              seq1 = seq_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);

              // If seq is odd, writer is active - spin briefly
              while (seq1 & 1) {
                  seq1 = seq_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
              }

              // Read the event
              size_t idx = playhead_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
              event_copy = events_[idx];  // Copy event data

              // Check if writer interrupted us
              seq2 = seq_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);

          } while (seq1 != seq2);  // Retry if write happened during read

          return event_copy;
      }

      // SCHEDULER THREAD - LOCK-FREE ADVANCE
      void advance_playhead() {
          playhead_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_release);
      }

      // CONTROL THREAD - MODIFY EVENT (May block on write_mutex_)
      void update_event(size_t index, const Event& new_event) {
          // Enforce: don't modify event under playhead
          size_t current = playhead_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
          if (index == current) {
              return; // Or throw, or queue for later
          }

          std::lock_guard lock(write_mutex_);

          // Increment seq (now odd - signals "writing")
          seq_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_release);

          // Modify the event
          events_[index] = new_event;

          // Increment seq again (now even - signals "done")
          seq_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_release);
      }
  };

  Performance Characteristics

  Scheduler thread (reader):
  - ✓ No locks ever taken
  - ✓ No blocking on mutexes
  - ✓ Bounded time (no indefinite spinning)
  - ✓ In common case (no concurrent write): just 2 atomic loads
  - ✓ If write is concurrent: retry is very fast (microseconds)

  Control thread (writer):
  - Takes mutex (blocks other writers)
  - Does NOT block scheduler thread
  - Slightly slower due to atomic increments

  Even Simpler: If Events Are Atomic-Copyable

  If your Event type is small enough (e.g., POD struct ≤ 16 bytes on x64), you can use this even simpler approach:

  class Sequencer {
  private:
      std::vector<std::atomic<Event>> events_;  // Each event is atomic!
      std::atomic<size_t> playhead_{0};
      std::mutex write_mutex_;  // Only for structural changes

  public:
      // SCHEDULER THREAD - WAIT-FREE READ
      Event get_current_event() const {
          size_t idx = playhead_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
          return events_[idx].load(std::memory_order_acquire);
      }

      void advance_playhead() {
          playhead_.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_release);
      }

      // CONTROL THREAD - UPDATE EVENT
      void update_event(size_t index, const Event& new_event) {
          size_t current = playhead_.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
          if (index == current) return;

          events_[index].store(new_event, std::memory_order_release);
      }

      // CONTROL THREAD - ADD/REMOVE EVENTS (structural changes)
      void push_back(const Event& event) {
          std::lock_guard lock(write_mutex_);
          events_.push_back(std::atomic<Event>(event));
      }
  };

  This requires: Event must be std::is_trivially_copyable

  For the Output Queue

  For pushing to output_, use a proven lock-free queue:

  #include <boost/lockfree/spsc_queue.hpp>  // Single-producer, single-consumer

  class Sequencer {
      boost::lockfree::spsc_queue<Event> output_{256};  // Fixed capacity

      // SCHEDULER THREAD
      void schedule_next_event() {
          Event event = get_current_event();
          output_.push(event);  // Lock-free, wait-free
          advance_playhead();
      }
  };

  Or if you want to avoid Boost, use std::atomic with indices for a ring buffer.




---------

2025-10-29

ALSA Sequencer Architecture Summary

  You asked about the ALSA sequencer design. Here are the key insights:

  Design Principles

  1. Separation of Concerns
    - Core only does: scheduling events + dispatching at right time
    - All MIDI processing happens in clients
    - Your MicroComposer already follows this! Your Sequencer schedules, handlers process.
  2. Client-Port Architecture
    - Each snd_seq_open() creates a client
    - Clients have multiple ports (connection points)
    - Ports have capability flags (read/write/subs)
  3. Subscription Model
    - Ports connect via subscriptions (like UNIX pipes)
    - Event destination: SND_SEQ_ADDRESS_SUBSCRIBERS routes to all connected clients
    - Enables flexible rerouting without restarting apps
  4. Dual-Queue Timing
    - Real-time queue: Wallclock (nanoseconds)
    - Tick queue: Musical time (tempo-based)
    - Your Sequencer uses real-time (steady_clock) - aligns with ALSA's real-time queue
  5. Isolated Memory Pools
    - Each client has separate input/output buffers
    - Prevents one client from starving others
    - Your thread-pool approach provides similar isolation

  What They Do Better

  - Subscription routing: Apps connect declaratively, runtime rerouting
  - Dual timing: Supports tempo changes without recalculating all timestamps
  - Zero-copy: Events stay in kernel until dispatched

  What You Do Better

  - Lock-free design: Your priority on scheduler thread
  - Type-safe events: C++ templates vs C unions
  - Modern concurrency: C++20 features vs kernel synchronization primitives

  Key Takeaway

  ALSA sequencer is a routing + scheduling service. Your MicroComposer is an event scheduling engine. They operate at different abstraction levels:

  - ALSA: System-wide MIDI patchbay
  - MicroComposer: Application-level sequencer with precise timing

  You can use ALSA's client/port system for I/O while keeping your superior scheduling logic.

  ---
  Resources for Deep Dive

  1. ALSA Sequencer API: https://alsa-project.org/alsa-doc/alsa-lib/seq.html
  2. Kernel Design: https://docs.kernel.org/sound/designs/
  3. Craig Sapp's Tutorials: https://ccrma.stanford.edu/~craig/articles/linuxmidi/
  4. libremidi Docs: https://celtera.github.io/libremidi/

  ---
  Questions for You

  Before I proceed with implementation:

  1. Scope: Start with just CLI MIDI output, or implement full GUI support immediately?
  2. Note-off strategy: Should I auto-generate note-off events when building sequences (Option C), or prefer a different approach?
  3. Port selection: Hard-code port 0 for testing, or build port discovery/selection UI?
  4. Dependencies: Okay to add libremidi as git submodule, or prefer CMake FetchContent?

  ---
  Answers to questions:
  1. Scope: start with just CLI MIDI output
  2. Note-off strategy: I propose that the Midi_event generates both the note-on and the note-off events in the Midi_event::to_midi_message(). If you wish, replace this method with Midi_event::to_midi_messages(), so that it returns 2 messages (one for note on and one for note off). So it may return a struct Midi_event::Message like this: ```
struct Message {
  libremidi::message note_on;
  libremidi::message note_off;
}
```. Then, the handler for Midi_event will send the note_on message, and then send the note_off message after the duration of the event has passed.
  SIDE NOTE: maybe we should just put a handler() function on every Event struct, since member functions are stored statically anyway and do not contribute to the size of the Event struct, and this will ensure that every type of event automatically has the right handler, remove the need to pass handlers to sequencers, make it more OOP etc. :)
  3. Port selection: build port discovery/selection UI.
  4. Dependencies: I would say just use CMake FetchContent (that seems easier to me), or do you have specific reasons to prefer a git submodule?
